WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.543
Concurrency adds risk because multiple threads or asynchronous tasks can observe and modify state at the same time.

2
00:00:06.543 --> 00:00:14.904
A design that is clear in single-threaded code may become fragile when callbacks, futures, thread pools, signal paths, or background workers enter

3
00:00:14.904 --> 00:00:15.631
the picture.

4
00:00:15.631 --> 00:00:18.539
Data races in C++ can create undefined behavior.

5
00:00:18.539 --> 00:00:25.809
That means the program result is not merely hard to predict; the language rules no longer provide the normal guarantees.

6
00:00:25.809 --> 00:00:33.079
For secure engineering, this matters because a data race near validation state, authorization state, object lifetime, or resource cleanup can

7
00:00:33.079 --> 00:00:35.624
undermine assumptions that other code depends on.

8
00:00:35.624 --> 00:00:39.622
Shared ownership should be minimized and documented where it is unavoidable.

9
00:00:39.622 --> 00:00:46.529
Immutable data, message passing, clear task boundaries, and ownership transfer can reduce the amount of state that needs synchronization.

10
00:00:46.529 --> 00:00:54.890
When mutable state is shared, the code should define who can access it, under what lock or atomic rule, and for how long.

11
00:00:54.890 --> 00:00:57.798
Synchronization choices should match the data being protected.

12
00:00:57.798 --> 00:01:02.887
Locks can protect complex invariants but can also introduce deadlocks if ordering is unclear.

13
00:01:02.887 --> 00:01:08.340
Atomics can be appropriate for narrow state but are not a universal replacement for locks.

14
00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:13.429
Queues and task systems can isolate ownership but require clear shutdown and backpressure behavior.

15
00:01:13.429 --> 00:01:18.518
Object lifetime becomes especially important when work crosses threads, callbacks, futures, or asynchronous APIs.

16
00:01:18.518 --> 00:01:23.607
A callback that captures a reference may run after the referenced object is gone.

17
00:01:23.607 --> 00:01:27.242
A background task may outlive the object that launched it.

18
00:01:27.242 --> 00:01:31.241
Shutdown paths should define cancellation, joining, ownership release, and error reporting.

19
00:01:31.241 --> 00:01:35.966
Review should trace shared data, synchronization, ownership transfer, shutdown behavior, and error paths.

20
00:01:35.966 --> 00:01:39.601
The goal is not to make every component lock-heavy.

21
00:01:39.601 --> 00:01:43.600
The goal is to make shared state intentional, bounded, and testable.
